{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed720058768/3/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e8686506\" lines=\"172-185\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered colored fragments into a left-right symmetric rectangle, jigsaw-style.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; remaining colors form small sprite fragments.\n- Optional roles: 4 singleton pixels of one color mark corners; horizontal bars mark top/bottom edges; paired vertical lines mark left/right edges.\n\nRule:\n- Collect all non-bg connected components as jigsaw pieces.\n- Determine output rectangle dimensions from total piece area.\n- Pack pieces into the rectangle so it is exactly mirror-symmetric (left-right) and same-color pieces never 4-touch.\n\nKey insight: Left-right symmetry plus the no-same-color-touching constraint make the assembly unique; corner/edge markers anchor it when present.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3a25b0d8\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes in a target object with colors from a source object's subsprites, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Two similar 8-connected objects share an outline color.\n- Source has colored subsprites in cavities; target has empty cavities.\n\nRule:\n- Find the two 8-connected objects.\n- Target = object with more hole area. Align subsprites to holes (rotation/flip if needed).\n- Fill each hole with its matched subsprite color; tight-crop.\n\nKey insight: Hole centers in the target are matched to source subsprite centers under the best rotation/flip transform.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8f3a5a89\" lines=\"22-36\">\n<description>\nPaint an inner border around the region reachable from a corner marker pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; wall color (blue) forms barriers dividing the grid.\n- A unique single pixel (magenta) sits on one corner — the start marker.\n- Some wall-colored pixels are structural (border-touching, enclosing regions); others are decorations.\n\nRule:\n- Flood-fill from the marker corner to find all reachable bg cells, blocked by structural walls.\n- Erase wall-colored decorations hidden behind structural walls (not reachable from marker side).\n- Paint orange (color 7) on every reachable bg cell adjacent to a wall or grid edge — forming a 1-cell inner border.\n\nKey insight: Only border-connected wall components act as real barriers; interior wall fragments are decorations to be removed.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"bf45cf4b\" lines=\"7-19\">\n<description>\nScale up a monochrome control sprite by stamping a polychrome tile at each filled pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background color.\n- Two foreground objects (8-connected): a monochrome rectangular sprite with bg holes (control), and a polychrome square tile (block).\n\nRule:\n- For each cell in the control sprite's bbox: if filled, stamp a copy of the block tile; if bg, leave a tile-sized bg region.\n- Output grid dimensions are (control_h × tile_h) by (control_w × tile_w).\n\nKey insight: The control sprite acts as a binary mask that gets \"pixel-scaled\" by the tile — each pixel expands to a full tile copy or empty space.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7c66cb00\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nDock two-tone sprites into color-matched horizontal bars with a color swap.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Top area: small two-tone sprites (outer color + inner color).\n- Below: horizontal bars spanning most of the row, each with a fill color and border color on edges.\n\nRule:\n- Match each sprite to the bar whose fill color equals the sprite's outer color.\n- Place the sprite at its original x-position, aligned to the bar's bottom.\n- Recolor: sprite outer → bar border, sprite inner → bar fill.\n- Erase original sprites from the top area.\n\nKey insight: Color matching is inverted — the sprite's dominant color identifies its target bar, then gets swapped to the bar's accent color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"aa4ec2a5\" lines=\"52-66\">\n<description>\nAdd red outlines around objects, and mark enclosed holes inside hollow objects.\n\nInput structure:\n- A mostly uniform background contains one or more 4-connected foreground objects.\n- Some objects may enclose 4-connected background holes inside their bbox.\n\nRule:\n- For every foreground component, draw a one-cell 8-neighborhood outline in red (2).\n- If a component has enclosed background holes, recolor the component to teal (8).\n- Fill those enclosed holes with magenta (6) before drawing the outline.\n\nKey insight: Holes are treated as part of the object for outlining, while only objects\nthat actually enclose holes get the teal/magenta interior recoloring.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8698868d\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nMatch sprites to frames by connected-hole count, then nest each sprite inside its frame.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = azure (8). Large hollow squares (frames) arranged in a grid; smaller filled squares (sprites) placed elsewhere.\n- Each frame and sprite has a distinct color. Sprites may contain holes (non-sprite-color pixels).\n\nRule:\n- Frames form a regular grid; some may be bg-colored (invisible).\n- Count connected hole regions inside each frame's interior and each sprite.\n- Pair each frame with the sprite sharing the same hole count.\n- Place the matched sprite inside the frame border; recolor sprite holes to the frame's color.\n\nKey insight: The matching key is the number of connected hole components, not hole area or color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a6f40cea\" lines=\"74-88\">\n<description>\nComplete partial paths inside a framed rectangle by projecting external color traces inward.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, a 1px rectangular frame (border color) with uniform interior fill.\n- Colored components outside the frame touching its edges.\n\nRule:\n- Output = the frame's interior, filled with the fill color.\n- Map each outside component's frame-touch points into interior coordinates.\n- Monochrome components become solid Manhattan stripes between mapped endpoints.\n- Two-color components become alternating-color Manhattan paths.\n\nKey insight: Endpoint colors of two-color traces anchor the alternating phase deterministically.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dbff022c\" lines=\"275-288\">\n<description>\nFill hollow sprites' interiors using a color-palette legend on the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several hollow sprites with distinct border colors.\n- A small 2×N or N×2 palette block sits on one grid edge; one row/column lists border colors, the other lists corresponding fill colors.\n\nRule:\n- Locate the palette legend on a grid edge and read it as a border→fill color mapping.\n- Find enclosed bg regions (holes) inside sprites that don't touch the grid boundary.\n- For each hole, identify the most common adjacent border color, look it up in the palette, and fill the hole with the mapped fill color.\n\nKey insight: The palette's edge position and orientation determine reading direction; border colors in the palette uniquely key into fill colors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a395ee82\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nStamp translated copies of a monochrome template guided by a singleton pixel layout.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A large monochrome shape (template, color T).\n- Singleton pixels elsewhere: exactly one is color T (layout origin); the rest share color O (overlap color).\n- Singletons sit on a uniform-spacing lattice.\n\nRule:\n- Convert each singleton's offset from the origin into coarse lattice units.\n- Place a template copy at each offset, scaled by template height/width.\n- The origin's copy is painted O instead of T.\n- Any pixel covered by two or more copies is also recolored to O.\n\nKey insight: The single T-colored singleton identifies both the origin position and the \"self\" copy, which is uniquely recolored.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"0934a4d8\" lines=\"75-90\">\n<description>\nReveal the rectangular patch hidden by the solid marker using the grid's reflection symmetries.\n\nInput structure:\n- A square colored pattern with one solid rectangular marker color occluding a patch.\n- The visible pattern has horizontal and vertical reflection symmetry on a large lower/right strip.\n- The top and left outer strips also expose transpose counterparts for cells not recoverable by those reflections.\n\nRule:\n- Find the unique color whose full extent is a solid rectangle; this rectangle is the output region.\n- Infer the reflected strip start as the smallest row/column offset whose lower/right strips are mirror-consistent, ignoring marker pixels.\n- For each marker pixel, copy the unique non-marker value in its horizontal/vertical reflection orbit; if that orbit is entirely hidden in the outer strip, use the transposed top/left-strip counterpart.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker is not part of the pattern; it covers a missing rectangle whose cells are still determined by the visible mirror or transpose copies elsewhere in the grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2b83f449\" lines=\"10-22\">\n<description>\nSimulate tokens falling through a pipe network after gates rotate open.\n\nInput structure:\n- Wall (0), bg (pipe fill), gate (horizontal 1×3 bars), token (small edge pixels).\n\nRule:\n- Rotate each gate into a vertical blocker; gate cells become bg.\n- Tokens drop downward through bg, choosing the extreme column for their side (left→min, right→max).\n- Tokens settle at true pipe ends (adjacent to wall/boundary), packing inward.\n\nKey insight: Tokens select drop columns by entry side and land at wall-bounded endpoints, ignoring blocker-bounded ones.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a251c730\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nStamp source sprites into a destination frame via color-matched singleton markers.\n\nInput structure:\n- Two bordered rectangles: source (multi-cell sprites) and destination (singleton markers).\n- Each sprite has an anchor pixel — its least-frequent color. Markers share these colors.\n\nRule:\n- Match each destination marker to the source sprite with the same anchor color.\n- Translate the sprite so its anchor overlaps the marker position.\n- Output is the destination rectangle, interior cleared before stamping.\n\nKey insight: The rarest color per sprite doubles as a template key and positioning anchor.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"16b78196\" lines=\"6-20\">\n<description>\nAssemble colored sprites into a central bar's notches, chaining successive pieces along fixed-width strips.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Largest monochrome component is a solid \"bar\" with boundary notches (bg holes opening to its bbox edge).\n- Several smaller colored sprites scattered around the bar.\n\nRule:\n- Each bar notch is filled by a root piece whose shape exactly covers the hole.\n- The root defines a strip (column-span or row-span) for its chain.\n- Successive pieces fill the bg cells inside the previous piece's bbox, locked to the same strip width.\n- Multiple chains grow independently from different bar notches.\n\nKey insight: The root piece's extent perpendicular to the chain direction locks the strip width for all subsequent pieces in that chain.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"15\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"faa9f03d\" lines=\"36-52\">\n<description>\nRepair a one-cell-thick colored line network by replacing singleton markers, filling short orthogonal gaps, and redirecting yellow-marked arms to the edge.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
